IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `euca-nouns`
IMPORT `euca-shared`

-- Section 203(d), (e), (f): the State "on" and "off" indicators. Layer 1: the law, objectively.
-- A State is "on" for a week when unemployment is high enough, by the insured unemployment test
-- of subsection (d) or (if the State law so provides) by the total unemployment trigger of
-- subsection (f). An "on" week followed by the rules of subsection (a) and (b) makes an extended
-- benefit period (euca-s203-period). Rates are percentage points: 5 means 5 per cent.
-- The relation: the State agency (s 203(e)(2)) and the Secretary (s 203(f)(3)) determine the
-- rates; the State holds the power to adopt the options in (d) and (f) by State law.
-- out of text: the rates themselves, the State law's adoption of an option and the date it
-- establishes, and the Secretary's regulations (s 203(e)(2)) and determinations (s 203(f)(3)):
-- plain inputs in the reading records declared in euca-nouns.

§ `Section 203(d), (e), (f): the indicators`

§§ `203(e): the rate of insured unemployment`

-- src:90 | “(e) [Rate of Insured Unemployment; Covered Employment] (1) For purposes of subsection (d), the term ‘rate of insured unemployment’ means the percentage arrived at by dividing—
-- src:91 | “(A) the average weekly number of individuals filing claims for regular compensation for weeks of unemployment with respect to the specified period, as determined on the basis of the reports made by the State agency to the Secretary, by
-- src:92 | “(B) the average monthly covered employment for the specified period.
@ref s 203(e)(1) (src:90-92)
GIVEN `average weekly number of individuals filing claims for regular compensation` IS A NUMBER
      `average monthly covered employment` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the rate of insured unemployment` `average weekly number of individuals filing claims for regular compensation` `average monthly covered employment` MEANS
    IF `average monthly covered employment` GREATER THAN 0
    THEN JUST ((`average weekly number of individuals filing claims for regular compensation` DIVIDED BY `average monthly covered employment`) TIMES 100)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:89 | For purposes of this subsection, the rate of insured unemployment for any thirteen-week period shall be determined by reference to the average monthly covered employment under the State law for the first four of the most recent six calendar quarters ending before the close of such period.
@ref s 203(d), closing paragraph, third sentence (src:89)
GIVEN `the six most recent calendar quarters ending before the close of the period, earliest first, as average monthly covered employment` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the average monthly covered employment for a thirteen-week period` `the six most recent calendar quarters ending before the close of the period, earliest first, as average monthly covered employment` MEANS
    IF count `the six most recent calendar quarters ending before the close of the period, earliest first, as average monthly covered employment` EQUALS 6
    THEN JUST (sum (take 4 `the six most recent calendar quarters ending before the close of the period, earliest first, as average monthly covered employment`) DIVIDED BY 4)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:93 | “(2) Determinations under subsection (d) shall be made by the State agency in accordance with regulations prescribed by the Secretary.
@ref s 203(e)(2) (src:93)
GIVETH A Party
`the party who makes the determinations under subsection (d)` MEANS `the State agency`

§§ `203(d): the insured unemployment test`

-- src:89 | Effective with respect to compensation for weeks of unemployment beginning after March 30, 1977 (or, if later, the date established pursuant to State law), the State may by law provide that the determination of whether there has been a State ‘on’ or ‘off’ indicator beginning or ending any extended benefit period shall be made under this subsection as if (i) paragraph (1) did not contain subparagraph (A) thereof, and (ii) the figure ‘5’ contained in subparagraph (B) thereof were ‘6’; except that, notwithstanding any such provision of State law, any week for which there would otherwise be a State ‘on’ indicator shall continue to be such a week and shall not be determined to be a week for which there is a State ‘off’ indicator.
@ref s 203(d), closing paragraph, first sentence (src:89)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the State law's 6 per cent option applies to the week` week reading MEANS
    CONSIDER reading's `date from which the State law adopts the 6 per cent option`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST adopted THEN week GREATER THAN (`the later of two dates` `30 March 1977` adopted)

-- The option covers weeks of unemployment "beginning after the date of enactment ... [Dec. 17, 2010]
-- (or, if later, the date established pursuant to State law), and ending on or before December 31,
-- 2013": the week must begin after the later date and its last day (6 days on) must be no later
-- than 31 December 2013.
-- src:89 | Effective with respect to compensation for weeks of unemployment beginning after the date of enactment of the Tax Relief, Unemployment Insurance Reauthorization, and Job Creation Act of 2010 [Dec. 17, 2010] (or, if later, the date established pursuant to State law), and ending on or before December 31, 2013, the State may by law provide that the determination of whether there has been a state [State] ‘on’ or ‘off’ indicator beginning or ending any extended benefit period shall be made under this subsection as if the word ‘two’ were ‘three’ in subparagraph (1)(A).
@ref s 203(d), closing paragraph, second sentence (src:89)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the State law's three-year comparison option of section 203(d) applies to the week` week reading MEANS
    CONSIDER reading's `date from which the State law adopts the three-year comparison option`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST adopted THEN
            week GREATER THAN (`the later of two dates` `17 December 2010` adopted)
        AND (week PLUS 6) AT MOST `31 December 2013`

-- src:86 | equaled or exceeded 120 per centum of the average of such rates for the corresponding thirteen-week period ending in each of the preceding two calendar years, and
@ref s 203(d)(1)(A) (src:86)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the average of such rates for the corresponding thirteen-week period in each of the preceding calendar years` week reading MEANS
    IF `the State law's three-year comparison option of section 203(d) applies to the week` week reading
    THEN (  reading's `rate for the corresponding thirteen-week period ending in the first preceding calendar year`
          + reading's `rate for the corresponding thirteen-week period ending in the second preceding calendar year`
          + reading's `rate for the corresponding thirteen-week period ending in the third preceding calendar year`) DIVIDED BY 3
    ELSE (  reading's `rate for the corresponding thirteen-week period ending in the first preceding calendar year`
          + reading's `rate for the corresponding thirteen-week period ending in the second preceding calendar year`) DIVIDED BY 2

-- src:86 | “(A) equaled or exceeded 120 per centum of the average of such rates for the corresponding thirteen-week period ending in each of the preceding two calendar years, and
@ref s 203(d)(1)(A) (src:86)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the rate equals or exceeds 120 per centum of the average of the corresponding rates` week reading MEANS
    reading's `rate of insured unemployment for the week and the preceding twelve weeks` AT LEAST
        (1.2 TIMES (`the average of such rates for the corresponding thirteen-week period in each of the preceding calendar years` week reading))

-- src:87 | “(B) equaled or exceeded 5 per centum.
@ref s 203(d)(1)(B) (src:87)
GIVEN reading IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the rate equals or exceeds 5 per centum` reading MEANS
    reading's `rate of insured unemployment for the week and the preceding twelve weeks` AT LEAST 5

-- src:85 | There is a State ‘on’ indicator for a week if the rate of insured unemployment under the State law for the period consisting of such week and the immediately preceding twelve weeks—
-- src:86 | “(A) equaled or exceeded 120 per centum of the average of such rates for the corresponding thirteen-week period ending in each of the preceding two calendar years, and
-- src:87 | “(B) equaled or exceeded 5 per centum.
@ref s 203(d)(1) (src:85-87)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`there is a State on indicator under paragraph (1) of section 203(d)` week reading MEANS
        `the rate equals or exceeds 120 per centum of the average of the corresponding rates` week reading
    AND `the rate equals or exceeds 5 per centum` reading

-- Under the option, the rate need only reach 6 per cent; but a week that would otherwise be "on"
-- under paragraph (1) stays "on" whatever State law says.
-- src:89 | the State may by law provide that the determination of whether there has been a State ‘on’ or ‘off’ indicator beginning or ending any extended benefit period shall be made under this subsection as if (i) paragraph (1) did not contain subparagraph (A) thereof, and (ii) the figure ‘5’ contained in subparagraph (B) thereof were ‘6’; except that, notwithstanding any such provision of State law, any week for which there would otherwise be a State ‘on’ indicator shall continue to be such a week and shall not be determined to be a week for which there is a State ‘off’ indicator.
@ref s 203(d), closing paragraph, first sentence (src:89)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`there is a State on indicator under section 203(d)` week reading MEANS
        `there is a State on indicator under paragraph (1) of section 203(d)` week reading
    OR  (    `the State law's 6 per cent option applies to the week` week reading
         AND reading's `rate of insured unemployment for the week and the preceding twelve weeks` AT LEAST 6)

-- src:88 | “(2) There is a State ‘off’ indicator for a week if, for the period consisting of such week and the immediately preceding twelve weeks, either subparagraph (A) or subparagraph (B) of paragraph (1) is not satisfied.
@ref s 203(d)(2) (src:88)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`there is a State off indicator under section 203(d)` week reading MEANS
    NOT (`there is a State on indicator under section 203(d)` week reading)

§§ `203(f): the alternative trigger on total unemployment`

-- src:94 | the State may by law provide that for purposes of beginning or ending any extended benefit period under this section—
@ref s 203(f)(1) (src:94)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS A `Total unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the alternative trigger applies to the week` week reading MEANS
        reading's `the State law adopts the alternative trigger`
    AND week GREATER THAN `6 March 1993`

-- src:100 | Effective with respect to compensation for weeks of unemployment beginning after the date of enactment of the Tax Relief, Unemployment Insurance Reauthorization, and Job Creation Act of 2010 [Dec. 17, 2010] (or, if later, the date established pursuant to State law), and ending on or before December 31, 2013, the State may by law provide that the determination of whether there has been a state [State] ‘on’ or ‘off’ indicator beginning or ending any extended benefit period shall be made under this subsection as if the word ‘either’ were ‘any’, the word ‘both’ were ‘all’, and the figure ‘2’ were ‘3’ in clause (1)(A)(ii).
@ref s 203(f)(2) (src:100)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS A `Total unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the State law's three-year comparison option of section 203(f) applies to the week` week reading MEANS
    CONSIDER reading's `date from which the State law adopts the three-year comparison option`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST adopted THEN
            week GREATER THAN (`the later of two dates` `17 December 2010` adopted)
        AND (week PLUS 6) AT MOST `31 December 2013`

-- src:96 | the average rate of total unemployment in such State (seasonally adjusted) for the period consisting of the most recent 3 months for which data for all States are published before the close of such week equals or exceeds 6.5 percent
@ref s 203(f)(1)(A)(i) (src:96)
GIVEN reading IS A `Total unemployment reading`
      percentage IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the average rate of total unemployment equals or exceeds the percentage` reading percentage MEANS
    reading's `average rate of total unemployment (seasonally adjusted) for the most recent 3 months for which data for all States are published` AT LEAST percentage

-- "for either (or both) of the corresponding 3-month periods ending in the 2 preceding calendar
-- years": at least one of the two comparisons is enough. Under the 2010 option "either" is "any",
-- "both" is "all" and "2" is "3": at least one of three.
-- src:97 | “(ii) the average rate of total unemployment in such State (seasonally adjusted) for the 3-month period referred to in clause (i) equals or exceeds 110 percent of such average rate for either (or both) of the corresponding 3-month periods ending in the 2 preceding calendar years; and
-- src:100 | the figure ‘2’ were ‘3’ in clause (1)(A)(ii).
@ref s 203(f)(1)(A)(ii) and (f)(2) (src:97 src:100)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS A `Total unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the average rate equals or exceeds 110 percent of the rate for a corresponding period in a preceding year` week reading MEANS
    LET rate MEANS reading's `average rate of total unemployment (seasonally adjusted) for the most recent 3 months for which data for all States are published` IN
            rate AT LEAST (1.1 TIMES reading's `average rate for the corresponding 3-month period ending in the first preceding calendar year`)
        OR  rate AT LEAST (1.1 TIMES reading's `average rate for the corresponding 3-month period ending in the second preceding calendar year`)
        OR  (    `the State law's three-year comparison option of section 203(f) applies to the week` week reading
             AND rate AT LEAST (1.1 TIMES reading's `average rate for the corresponding 3-month period ending in the third preceding calendar year`))

-- The test of clauses (i) and (ii), at a percentage the caller names. Section 203(f)(1)(A)(i)
-- names 6.5 percent; section 202(b)(3)(B) substitutes 8 percent to define a high unemployment
-- period, and asks whether a period "would be in effect" if the test were applied, whether or not
-- the State law has adopted the alternative trigger.
-- src:95 | “(A) there is a State ‘on’ indicator for a week if—
-- src:96 | “(i) the average rate of total unemployment in such State (seasonally adjusted) for the period consisting of the most recent 3 months for which data for all States are published before the close of such week equals or exceeds 6.5 percent, and
-- src:97 | “(ii) the average rate of total unemployment in such State (seasonally adjusted) for the 3-month period referred to in clause (i) equals or exceeds 110 percent of such average rate for either (or both) of the corresponding 3-month periods ending in the 2 preceding calendar years; and
@ref s 203(f)(1)(A) (src:95-97)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS A `Total unemployment reading`
      percentage IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the alternative trigger test is met at the percentage` week reading percentage MEANS
        `the average rate of total unemployment equals or exceeds the percentage` reading percentage
    AND `the average rate equals or exceeds 110 percent of the rate for a corresponding period in a preceding year` week reading

-- src:96 | equals or exceeds 6.5 percent
@ref s 203(f)(1)(A)(i) (src:96)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the percentage in section 203(f)(1)(A)(i)` MEANS 6.5

-- src:95 | “(A) there is a State ‘on’ indicator for a week if—
@ref s 203(f)(1)(A) (src:95)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS A `Total unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`there is a State on indicator under section 203(f)` week reading MEANS
        `the alternative trigger applies to the week` week reading
    AND `the alternative trigger test is met at the percentage` week reading `the percentage in section 203(f)(1)(A)(i)`

-- src:98 | “(B) there is a State ‘off’ indicator for a week if either the requirements of clause (i) or clause (ii) of subparagraph (A) are not satisfied.
@ref s 203(f)(1)(B) (src:98)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      reading IS A `Total unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`there is a State off indicator under section 203(f)` week reading MEANS
        `the alternative trigger applies to the week` week reading
    AND (NOT (`the alternative trigger test is met at the percentage` week reading `the percentage in section 203(f)(1)(A)(i)`))

-- src:101 | “(3) For purposes of this subsection, determinations of the rate of total unemployment in any State for any period (and of any seasonal adjustment) shall be made by the Secretary.
@ref s 203(f)(3) (src:101)
GIVETH A Party
`the party who determines the rate of total unemployment and any seasonal adjustment` MEANS `the Secretary of Labor`

§§ `203(d) and (f) together: the State on indicator for a week`

-- Under (f)(1), notwithstanding State law, a week that would otherwise be "on" (under (d)) stays
-- "on". So a week is "on" if either test says so; it is "off" if neither does.
-- src:99 | Notwithstanding the provision of any State law described in this paragraph, any week for which there would otherwise be a State ‘on’ indicator shall continue to be such a week and shall not be determined to be a week for which there is a State ‘off’ indicator.
@ref s 203(f)(1), last sentence (src:99)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      insured IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
      total IS A `Total unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`there is a State on indicator for the week` week insured total MEANS
        `there is a State on indicator under section 203(d)` week insured
    OR  `there is a State on indicator under section 203(f)` week total

-- src:99 | Notwithstanding the provision of any State law described in this paragraph, any week for which there would otherwise be a State ‘on’ indicator shall continue to be such a week and shall not be determined to be a week for which there is a State ‘off’ indicator.
@ref s 203(f)(1), last sentence (src:99)
GIVEN week IS A DATE
      insured IS AN `Insured unemployment reading`
      total IS A `Total unemployment reading`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`there is a State off indicator for the week` week insured total MEANS
    NOT (`there is a State on indicator for the week` week insured total)
